<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Exploring Our Global Community – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring Our Global Community</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> A Dream Trip Around the World</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Evaluate informational content from various sources. Reading: Summarize discussions from academic texts.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can evaluate information from travel articles and summarize cultural learning experiences related to other countries (e.g., "I read an article comparing the preservation efforts at the National Museum of Archaeology and Ethnology in Guatemala City and the Ixchel Museum of Indigenous Textiles and Clothing. Both emphasize the importance of preserving Mayan history and cultural identity.")
Can summarize discussions on intercultural exchange in academic texts (e.g., "The text explained that travel promotes global understanding by exposing people to different cultures, allowing people to experience new perspectives and challenge their own assumptions.")
Can discuss insights gained from informational texts on global travel (e.g., "The article suggests that learning a second language while traveling opens up new opportunities for communication, helps travelers understand local customs, and enhances their overall cultural experience.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a world map or images of diverse, breathtaking global destinations (e.g., pyramids, ancient cities, unique waterfalls, stunning beaches, majestic mountains). Students are asked to quickly identify the places and share which one looks like the most exciting destination for an adventure. Teacher prompts: "If you could visit any of these places, where would you go and why?" or "What's the most *breathtaking* cultural experience you've ever heard about?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the use of the second conditional and target vocabulary by responding to the warm-up question. "If I could travel anywhere, I would visit Egypt to explore the *pyramids*. I think it would be the *most unforgettable experience*." Teacher also models skimming for gist by quickly looking over a sample text related to travel. Teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of modal verbs: "If I *could* travel around the world, I *would* visit Egypt." (Stress pattern: If I *could* TRAVEL aROUND the WORLD, I *would* VISIT EGYPT.)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'If I could hike in the mountains, I would go to Nepal,' am I talking about a real trip or a dream trip?" (Expected answer: A dream trip/hypothetical)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'breathtaking' view something boring or something amazing that takes your breath away?" (Expected answer: Amazing/stunning)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher provides students with an authentic travel article titled: "Unforgettable Journeys: Exploring Cultural Diversity and Global Understanding." The article discusses different types of `travel` experiences, `cultural` exchange programs, and the `benefits` of broadening one's `perspectives`. It includes examples of destinations known for rich `traditions`, mentions `pyramids`, `beaches`, and `mountains`, and integrates `second conditional` for hypothetical scenarios (e.g., "If you *could experience* a year-long Exchange Program, you *would develop* incredible skills"), `superlatives` for comparisons (e.g., "the *most enriching* adventure"), and `past continuous` to describe traveler experiences (e.g., "While they *were exploring* the remote villages, they *were learning* about ancient customs").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading and evaluating informational content from travel articles to understand cultural learning and global exchange.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Reading for gist and specific information):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the article quickly (1-2 minutes) and answer: "What is the main purpose of this article? (a) To advertise travel agencies, (b) To inform about the personal benefits of cultural travel, (c) To describe the most dangerous travel destinations."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students re-read the article for details and mark the following statements as True or False:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Learning a second language while traveling *enhances* the overall cultural experience."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The article suggests that all `adventure` `trips` involve `hiking` in `mountains`."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"According to the text, global `understanding` is gained by experiencing `different` `perspectives`."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match phrases from the article describing a destination with the specific benefit of visiting it.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice evaluating and summarizing informational text using `second conditional`, `superlatives`, and `past continuous` while reinforcing target vocabulary: `travel`, `culture`, `experience`, `diversity`, `adventure`, `destination`, `trip`, `pyramids`, `beaches`, `mountains`, `world`, `waterfall`, `wonders`, `understanding`, `perspectives`, `skills`, `benefits`, `Exchange Program`, `traditions`, `explore`, `compare`, `travel`, `visit`, `experience`, `learn`, `enjoy`, `hike`, `encounter`, `enhance`, `develop`, `meet`, `understand`, `broaden`, `narrate`, `dream`, `reflect`, `gain`, `discover`, `share`, `breathtaking`, `global`, `romantic`, `natural`, `diverse`, `cultural`, `beautiful`, `unforgettable`, `personal`, `year-long`, `enriching`, `different`, `unique`, `educational`, `stunning`, `exciting`, `magnificent`, `remote`, `distant`, `traditional`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Reading for Grammar and Vocabulary):</b> Students complete a gap-fill exercise using sentences extracted directly from the article. The gaps will focus on target vocabulary (e.g., `diversity`, `enriching`, `perspectives`) and grammatical structures (e.g., `second conditional` verbs like "would visit," `superlatives` like "most beautiful," and `past continuous` verbs like "was exploring"). For example: "If I __________ (can, visit) any `waterfall`, I __________ (go) to Iguazu Falls. While I __________ (travel), I __________ (learn) about local `traditions`."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Summarizing Key Details):</b> In pairs, students re-read specific paragraphs of the article. They then choose three key phrases or sentences from their assigned paragraph that highlight the `benefits` of `cultural` `exchange` or `global` `understanding`. They should aim to rephrase these in their own words, using at least one `superlative` or `second conditional` structure. For example, after reading about an Exchange Program: "The article highlights that a `year-long` `Exchange Program` is the `most unique` way to `develop` `personal skills`. If I `could participate`, I `would gain` new `perspectives`."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Checking and Discussion):</b> Students compare their gap-fill answers and their summarized key phrases with another pair. They `discuss` any discrepancies and justify their choices by referring back to the text. They also `share` which parts of the article they found `most exciting` or `educational`. Pragmatic focus: "What is one tradition you found interesting *in the article* while reading about [a specific destination]?" "How was your experience reading this article in regards to changing your `perspective` on `travel`?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Group Travel Article Evaluation and Summary.</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group is tasked with evaluating the informational content of the provided article and summarizing its main arguments regarding the `benefits` of `global` `travel` and `cultural` `exchange`. They must identify and list the `top three benefits` discussed in the article for "A Dream Trip Around the World" and support each benefit with evidence/examples directly from the text. They should present their findings to the class in a short summary (2-3 minutes per group).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Groups collaboratively re-read the article, highlighting or noting down key points related to `cultural diversity` and `personal growth` through `travel`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. They discuss and `compare` their findings, deciding on the `three most important benefits` the article presents for someone planning a `dream trip`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. For each benefit, they locate specific textual evidence or examples (e.g., mention of an `Exchange Program`, a specific `destination`, or a type of `adventure`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Groups prepare a brief oral summary to share with the class, ensuring they incorporate `second conditional` (e.g., "If you *could explore* this culture, you *would understand* the `diversity`"), `superlatives` (e.g., "This article describes `travel` as the *most enriching* `experience`"), and `past continuous` (e.g., "The article highlights that while people *were traveling*, they *were gaining* new `perspectives`") from the `recommended language content`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. Sociolinguistic aspect: Students are encouraged to ensure everyone in the group has input and takes part in the discussion and presentation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussions and presentations, focusing on the ability to evaluate informational content and summarize discussions from the text. A quick peer assessment checklist for presentation content.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their "Top 3 Benefits of Global Travel and Cultural Exchange" based on the article, supported by textual evidence. Students also hand in a written summary of their findings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to accurately evaluate and summarize the main arguments of the informational text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear identification of `benefits` and supporting evidence from the article.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., `diversity`, `perspectives`, `enriching`, `understanding`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempted use of `second conditional`, `superlatives`, and `past continuous` during presentation and in written summary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and equitable participation within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the `most important` cultural insight or new idea I gained from today's reading about global `travel`?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did my group summarize and evaluate the information from the text today?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategy (skimming, scanning, close reading) did you find `most helpful` today for `understanding` the `diverse` `cultures` discussed in the article?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has today's reading broadened your `perspectives` and `understanding` of our `global` community?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well today? Students engaged well with the authentic travel article and demonstrated good initial comprehension. The group task encouraged collaboration and allowed for the integration of target grammar. Students were able to identify and summarize key `benefits` of `travel`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled to consistently integrate the `second conditional` and `past continuous` in their summaries; more specific examples and sentence starters might be useful. I need to explicitly check if they practiced stress on multisyllabic words like "adVENture" and "des-ti-NA-tion" when reading parts of the text or presenting.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), we will build on the `insights` and `vocabulary` from today's reading. We will actively `discuss` our own `dream trips` and hypothetical `cultural encounters`, using the structures and `perspectives` we've gained to `share` our thoughts more fluently."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>